{"type": "say", "text": "Good morning, everyone. Let's get started. We have 10 days to serve our response to the plaintiff's document demand, and we need to make some strategic decisions about how we handle the customer-support files, internal spreadsheets, and source-code materials. The plaintiff has already signaled they'll challenge any substantial withholding or restrictions on access. I'd like to hear from each of you on what you're seeing before we lock in our approach. Who wants to start?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "I need to flag something critical before we go further on the source-code materials. After the draft response circulated, I confirmed with the client's privacy office that their source-code license agreements prohibit any downloadable production of the code. The client requires that source code be made available for inspection only — in a secure review room, restricted to outside counsel, with no employee access and no downloads permitted. I had not yet relayed this confirmation to the litigation team, so I'm raising it now. This directly affects our production approach for the source-code materials and means the current draft's proposal for ordinary document production of that category is not viable."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF1", "text": "I completed a review of the custodian export this morning and found exactly 86 spreadsheets containing patient names or diagnoses. The production plan must include de-identified versions of those spreadsheets and redact all patient identifiers, because the existing confidentiality order is not a HIPAA-qualified protective order. Because that order is not HIPAA-qualified, we need to file a motion for a modified or supplemental protective order before any of those 86 spreadsheets can be produced. This has not yet been added to the draft response, and it affects the spreadsheet category directly."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Yes, Elena — I'll prepare a declaration identifying all 86 affected spreadsheets, including a summary of the types of patient identifiers and diagnostic information they contain, to support the supplemental protective order motion. I'll have a draft declaration and an exhibit list with custodian, file name, and date range for each spreadsheet circulating by end of day tomorrow alongside the revised response and motion. One thing I want to flag for the motion drafting: the justification should emphasize that the existing confidentiality order's lack of HIPAA-qualified status is not something we can cure through redaction alone — even de-identified versions may still fall within HIPAA's scope depending on the level of detail in the diagnostic fields, so the motion should request a qualified protective order that covers both the redacted and any unredacted versions if the court later orders fuller production. I'll note that distinction in the declaration so the motion team can address it."}
```

```json
{"type": "say", "text": "Elena, I'll reach out to the client's privacy office today to get formal documentation of the source-code license-agreement inspection-only requirement — specifically the contract provisions that prohibit downloads and restrict access to outside counsel in a secure review room. I'll aim to have that documentation ready to attach as an exhibit to our response by end of day tomorrow alongside everything else. One thing I want to flag for the motion and response drafting team: the justification for the source-code inspection-only restriction should be framed as a contractual compliance obligation, not merely a preference — the license agreements create a binding prohibition on downloadable production, so we're not voluntarily restricting access; we're legally constrained from producing it any other way. That framing will be important if the plaintiff challenges the restriction, because it shifts the analysis from whether our proposed restriction is reasonable to whether the court can compel us to breach our license agreements. I'll also make sure the documentation from the privacy office clearly identifies the specific license provisions at issue so the court can see the exact contractual language. If there's nothing else on the source-code category, I'll get started on coordinating with the privacy office."}
```

